At a high level, the difference comes down to the state of the data and the intended use case.
A data warehouse is a highly structured, curated repository optimized for business intelligence, while a data lake is a flexible, scalable storage pool for raw data optimized for exploration.

Technical Breakdown :-
A. Schema Breakdown :- 
A warehouse uses schema-on-write (ETL). We have to rigorously model, clean, and transform the data to fit rigid relational tables before it lands.
A data lake uses schema-on-read (ELT). We dump raw data—structured, JSON, logs, or even media—into object storage like S3, applying the schema only when we actually query it.

B. Cost and Scalability :-
Warehouses typically use expensive, specialized storage optimized for fast joins. Lakes use cheap, decoupled object storage, making it cost-effective to store petabytes of data indefinitely.

My decision in terms of buisness context :- 
In practice, they serve different teams. I would route data to a warehouse if business analysts need sub-second, reliable performance for Tableau dashboards or regulatory reporting.
I would point my data scientists to the data lake so they have access to the raw, unaggregated features they need to train machine learning models.

What if i need to design a data platform today in 2026? (Important)
if I were designing a greenfield data platform today, I wouldn't strictly separate them.
I would lean toward a Data Lakehouse architecture. By using open table formats like Apache Iceberg, Delta Lake, or Hudi on top of our data lake,
we can bring the ACID transactions, schema enforcement, and BI performance of a warehouse directly to our cheap object storage.

